// Relativization / partition-safety, `count == 0` (the EMPTY-own-set edge).
//
// A Read is permitted iff THIS principal has ZERO of its own Logins in the last
// hour ("never logged in"). The event schema pins `callerPrincipal`
// universally, so the count ranges over this principal's own events only. This
// is the inverse of 1157 (which pushes a principal OVER a threshold): here the
// key-local set is EMPTY, and a foreign key's events must not push the count
// OFF zero. The empty-own-set path in the relativized count encoding is only
// reached in this shape.
//
// The count body does not correlate on user (`Login{ input.user: _ }`), so the
// pin is the only thing confining the count. Two pairs, each with/without the
// foreign events:
//
//   PERMIT pair — alice has NO own Login; a foreign principal's Logins must not
//   count:
//     trace_1: BOB Login, BOB Login, alice Read.
//     trace_2:                       alice Read.
//   Correct (key-local): alice's own count = 0 → n == 0 → PERMIT in both. Under
//   (incorrect) global semantics trace_1 would count bob's two logins
//   (n == 2 ≠ 0) → DENY, diverging from trace_2.
//
//   DENY pair — alice HAS an own Login, so her count is not zero; the foreign
//   login is inert:
//     trace_3: alice Login, BOB Login, alice Read.
//     trace_4: alice Login,            alice Read.
//   Correct: alice's own count = 1 → n == 0 false → DENY in both. Confirms the
//   count DOES see alice's own event (is not stuck at 0), not vacuously permit.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    exists (n: Long). (
        (count for (t: Timepoint). where (
            formerly within 1h (Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: _ } && tp(t))
        )) == n && n == 0
    )
};
